    "body": "Shein has agreed to pay $100 million to settle a Federal Trade Commission investigation into delivery delays and other customer complaints, Reuters reported. The payment would equal 44% of the fast-fashion retailer ’s second-quarter profit.\nWhat’s Related\nConsumers filed more than 12,000 complaints about Shein involving purchases worth more than $11 million between January 2020 and June 2026, according to FTC records. Some customers said packages marked as delivered never arrived and that customer service did not resolve their complaints.\nShein, founded in China and headquartered in Singapore, told Reuters it had “reached a constructive outcome with the FTC” and would share more information soon. An FTC spokesperson declined to comment.\nThe company had warned in July filings for its Hong Kong initial public offering that the investigation could result in significant fines. Reuters did not detail how the $100 million would be distributed or what changes Shein would be required to make.\nRELATED STORIES: Walmart to Pay Drivers $100 Million in Settlement Over Misleading Pay | Amazon to Pay $8.25 Million in Delivery Lawsuit Settlement\nSo many complaints\nThe complaints include reports submitted to both the FTC and the Better Business Bureau. They raised questions about missing orders and how Shein handled customers who disputed delivery records.\nThe complaint totals also cover several years and include issues beyond shipping , so they should not be read as a count of confirmed delivery failures.\nAlong with its own clothing, Shein sells products from outside merchants through its online marketplace. Those listings include toys, household appliances, and home decor.\nThe FTC means business\nThe FTC has previously pursued online retailers over shipping delays and refunds. Its Mail, Internet, or Telephone Order Merchandise Rule requires sellers to have a reasonable basis for advertised shipping times. When no time is stated, sellers generally must ship within 30 days.\nIf a seller cannot ship on time, it must let customers choose to agree to a delay or cancel for a refund. The FTC says gift cards and store credit do not qualify as refunds for merchandise that was never shipped.\nThose requirements provide context for the agency’s work on delivery complaints, although Reuters’ report did not identify the specific provisions underlying the Shein settlement.\nIn 2020, Fashion Nova agreed to a $9.3 million settlement after the FTC alleged it failed to meet shipping promises and improperly issued gift cards instead of refunds. In 2023, Hey Dude agreed to pay $1.95 million to settle allegations involving shipping delays, refunds and the suppression of negative reviews.",
